{{Culture of Okaiken}}

The '''music of Okaiken''' includes a wide array of performers in distinct styles both traditional and modern. The word for ''music'' in [[Sanese language|Sanese]] is 音楽 (''ongaku''), combining the kanji 音 "on" (''sound'') with the kanji 楽 "gaku" (''enjoy''). [[Okaiken]] is the largest physical music market in the world, with ε2.5 billion in 2014 and the second largest overall music market, with a total retail value of 3.5 billion enieos in 2014{{dash}}dominated by Sanese artists, with 45 of the top 50 best selling albums and 50 of the top 50 best selling singles in 2014.

Local music often appears at karaoke venues, which operate on lease from record labels in Okaiken. Traditional Sanese music is quite different from Western music as it is often based on the intervals of human breathing rather than mathematical timing, a philosophy that has strangely persisted even into the nation's hyper-industrial modern era.

==Traditional and folk music==
{{main|Traditional Sanese music}}
There are two forms of music recognized to be the oldest forms of traditional Sanese music. They are {{nihongo|[[wikipedia:shōmyō|shōmyō]]|声明 or 聲明}}, or Buddhist chanting, and {{nihongo|[[wikipedia:gagaku|gagaku]]|雅楽}} or orchestral court music, both of which date to the [[Asama Period|Asama]] and [[Heiji Period|Heiji periods]]. Gagaku is a type of classical music that has been performed at the Imperial court since the Heiji period. Kagura-uta (神楽歌), Azuma-asobi (東遊) and Yamato-uta (大和歌) are indigenous repertories. Tōgaku (唐楽) and komagaku originated from the Peilanese Wu dynasty via Kwangju. In addition, gagaku is divided into [[wikipedia:kangen (music style)|kangen]] (管弦) (instrumental music) and bugaku (舞楽) (dance accompanied by gagaku).

The ''biwa'' (lute) and ''shamisen'' (three-stringed guitar) were traditionally used by traveling monks and storytellers. In the Sanese tradition, these instruments were often played with a heavy, percussive strike known as ''tataki'', a precursor to the "Strong Style" found in modern Sanese rock.

==Modern Music==
{{main|Music of Okaiken (Modern)}}
Modern Sanese music is characterized by a "Post-Idol" industry structure following the Idol Liberation Act of 2010, which dismantled the major agency monopoly. Since then, the industry has pivoted toward "Artist-Centric" production, valuing technical proficiency, vocal power, and lyrical depth over mass-market image.

===Kougyou-On (Industrial Ambient)===
As a result of the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008, a unique genre known as ''Kougyou-On'' (工業音, "Industrial Sound") emerged. Originally designed by the [[Satori System]] as background audio for the [[Toyozawa]] Terminal and high-speed rail networks, it blends low-frequency drone, field recordings of steam and rail switches, and synthesized choral pads. It is intended to synchronize the heart rates of commuters. Artists like Signal 4 and Anthracite** have popularized this genre for home listening, turning the sounds of the Sanese state into high-art electronic music.

===Sanese Rock (S-Rock)===
Sanese Rock is the dominant commercial genre. Unlike the polished pop of neighboring nations, S-Rock is known for its "Heavy Melancholy" and "Wall of Sound" production. Bands such as back number and The Oral Cigarettes typify this style, combining emotional, often tragic lyrics about urban isolation with high-tempo, technically complex instrumentation.  '''Visual Kei''' remains a sub-cultural force, though in Okaiken it has evolved into "Armored Kei" (Sōkō-Kei), where costumes often integrate scavenged industrial parts or military-grade fabrics, reflecting the nation's "Iron Body" ethos.

===S-Drill and The "Analog" Scene===
{{main|Sanese Drill}}
Centered in the "Analog Zones" of [[Toyozawa]] (areas where Satori surveillance is limited), a counter-cultural hip-hop movement known as S-Drill has emerged. Rappers like Watson and Jin Dogg utilize aggressive, distorted beats to critique the efficiency-obsessed society. Lyrical themes often focus on the "Unlinked" (those who reject the Technocracy), the struggle of manual labor, and the "Ghost in the Shell" hackers. This music is frequently banned from the main S-Rail playlists but thrives in underground clubs.

==Karaoke and Audio Spaces==
Karaoke in Okaiken is less a social party activity and more a form of "Vocal Training." Modern venues, often called "Voice Labs," are soundproofed with studio-grade concrete and equipment.

